52a6524f23590ca185fc6ec69463d0fa352e91e6017a39710272916"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109734" :question/id #uuid "3d33294f-5875-5283-bf3a-299f02db3b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-109734" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [2,5].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [2,5].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [2,5],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109734/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109773/selection/ee5c6652b0765a1ad46e678ffb3855295dca02e140f1982b63f61c1462faddc9" :answer/id #uuid "b7f74f39-6138-5fcc-a078-9d3bde9c532b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109773/selection/ca70f9d10ed0d936401231cd9fbe52f0c2e098194bf997e208edb817c861e191" :answer/id #uuid