 the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={sin}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156978/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156978"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection/e55c4f9a641b019bc5cd9cee07624b7a8a0b9fe5bcf94d4b1172e2c6ddd3231f" :answer/id #uuid "782837c6-be93-5c2b-8c3c-9808561f4770" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection/6d93c6eaa76aa57bf66cc6915d490dba023d896dbb1e2de1416f2943ff9f957f" :answer/id #uuid "175157c2-2e24-597a-8c7f-4ab4ba084122" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection/c432f17092872a01c1c3ec5ca4378f0f74ae9a4262088003c927a960886c3d4b" :answer/id #uuid "146d1577-c1ea-56a0-9a89-32f73d0d4dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection/54eb1d4ea039263c9bdaf56f5e40882e6746ef83d31e7b60c50a57cd81d87370" :answer/id #uuid "5174fa55-050d-5327-a34d-df1f44757514" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{z}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection/16cbed731ac5bc91b8bf0a0d0b22f158a6a694fb065ed8d4db327607616ca47b" :answer/id #uuid "52cab525-f917-5b33-b0b8-cb2d10c4097e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "76962e7f-76d0-5db3-b7e6-6c8507a6f395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161030/selection/16cbed731ac5bc91b8bf0a0d0b22f158a6a694fb065ed8d4db327607616ca47b" :answer-field/choices ["q-161030/selection/e55c4f9a641b019bc5cd9cee07624b7a8a0b9fe5bcf94d4b1172e2c6ddd3231f" "q-161030/selection/6d93c6eaa76aa57bf66cc6915d490dba023d896dbb1e2de1416f2943ff9f957f" "q-161030/selection/c432f17092872a01c1c3ec5ca4378f0f74ae9a4262088003c927a960886c3d4b" "q-161030/selection/54eb1d4ea039263c9bdaf56f5e40882e6746ef83d31e7b60c50a57cd81d87370" "q-161030/selection/16cbed731ac5bc91b8bf0a0d0b22f158a6a694fb065ed8d4db327607616ca47b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161030" :question/id #uuid "49af9a61-4963-5431-b77a-d640902c4765" :question/math-academy-id "q-161030" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1].$ What is the second component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161030/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161030"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection/3153abda00ea9ac8d0fd5547564d4a0a8f9cc98394f3bc5be7387dc979a0e9e2" :answer/id #uuid "3e0eccc0-4369-5250-b1e2-90b150cd772e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection/6ed7541656a78e913aa4a29fcc63f14df4ad943c9afe513ee16462bc07eb26b0" :answer/id #uuid "77f33888-294a-5a6d-b9db-e199c547cd23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection/9ab48649008bac28ee0df62c64cc3060c4fde298bac9feaef461ab0fe2acf074" :answer/id #uuid "6089b59a-e049-54e2-aedc-1cc5e0b7995f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection/ea1e338de2a0cb8a640a44950398210d1ca222223a655996523a7e9a1815ad8e" :answer/id #uuid "6a81dc70-d8d2-5235-909f-42fd500ed39c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection/3e66cca8fe407613945def57e4eeac946e11e0947918728e7e2d134ed21a970c" :answer/id #uuid "7d2c4670-3794-5753-94a3-9d6ffe783e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f72cfa62-153a-5d29-905c-4b714d529eb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161061/selection/3e66cca8fe407613945def57e4eeac946e11e0947918728e7e2d134ed21a970c" :answer-field/choices ["q-161061/selection/3153abda00ea9ac8d0fd5547564d4a0a8f9cc98394f3bc5be7387dc979a0e9e2" "q-161061/selection/6ed7541656a78e913aa4a29fcc63f14df4ad943c9afe513ee16462bc07eb26b0" "q-161061/selection/9ab48649008bac28ee0df62c64cc3060c4fde298bac9feaef461ab0fe2acf074" "q-161061/selection/ea1e338de2a0cb8a640a44950398210d1ca222223a655996523a7e9a1815ad8e" "q-161061/selection/3e66cca8fe407613945def57e4eeac946e11e0947918728e7e2d134ed21a970c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161061" :question/id #uuid "1fd05543-77d3-5883-9430-aa64bbd0f377" :question/math-academy-id "q-161061" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi /4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161061/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161061"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection/07efa0ff5885fb59a7cb0366c3e2a5cfad30a1520a95421db8c53a8b2e583777" :answer/id #uuid "4f94326b-17f1-558b-b9b0-47872b935c65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection/9d74eb82d04cda9ff4b17bb80e6fc1cc1c09fb0b3a30039f476e3f00d3ad20f7" :answer/id #uuid "7a15cf79-b0b4-5bee-a738-38c97cb93daf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection/dd9fd34b513ca8947d42ee821564e1a9da5af743cc88844fa7bf07c452b1d07d" :answer/id #uuid "bcb990c6-bf6e-5223-98be-3c7a32b025c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" :answer/id #uuid "3ce600dd-4a52-5663-87a2-88fa29d8d54b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" :answer/id #uuid "35d8aec9-08d4-552e-adab-16dc04f9f331" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a5d101-527a-5b26-95d6-1df523e911e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161072/selection/9d74eb82d04cda9ff4b17bb80e6fc1cc1c09fb0b3a30039f476e3f00d3ad20f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-161072/selection/07efa0ff5885fb59a7cb0366c3e2a5cfad30a1520a95421db8c53a8b2e583777" "q-161072/selection/9d74eb82d04cda9ff4b17bb80e6fc1cc1c09fb0b3a30039f476e3f00d3ad20f7" "q-161072/selection/dd9fd34b513ca8947d42ee821564e1a9da5af743cc88844fa7bf07c452b1d07d" "q-161072/selection/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" "q-161072/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161072" :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161072/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161072"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection/67ea75d0fe51ce41050ac180c6c253e471ec7dde4a5214947e71e4b45cea26d7" :answer/id #uuid "1fdd035d-b276-5cbf-b812-bccb5b6e2fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection/401e787f4118601e1313d29bcdb93d3e713e830221ae7cce8c4850cb5e7c62ce" :answer/id #uuid "eb63ff44-9243-5879-9176-5e6cf970ac6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{z}{({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection/69f3bac589b95a5693da5c7ba1af4e870140b650d0352ed6f6791aa3a30ff143" :answer/id #uuid "68b78e65-4151-5712-b5c0-b0dc3c601c13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection/723cc8d2265306cecfb5760f4869e4d4498297de188c32a1bad0a4a80174c18d" :answer/id #uuid "741877b6-0d83-5bb3-944f-8a02758d21d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection/a4c038b4a2279610154b317b49ae880e7b849790b1d70eac76d2e0745dfc5825" :answer/id #uuid "a3957991-6dd9-5f30-a940-bd077d74358d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "62b4a7d6-5c4e-5232-84cc-995ba9d8a1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161063/selection/723cc8d2265306cecfb5760f4869e4d4498297de188c32a1bad0a4a80174c18d" :answer-field/choices ["q-161063/selection/67ea75d0fe51ce41050ac180c6c253e471ec7dde4a5214947e71e4b45cea26d7" "q-161063/selection/401e787f4118601e1313d29bcdb93d3e713e830221ae7cce8c4850cb5e7c62ce" "q-161063/selection/69f3bac589b95a5693da5c7ba1af4e870140b650d0352ed6f6791aa3a30ff143" "q-161063/selection/723cc8d2265306cecfb5760f4869e4d4498297de188c32a1bad0a4a80174c18d" "q-161063/selection/a4c038b4a2279610154b317b49ae880e7b849790b1d70eac76d2e0745dfc5825"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161063" :question/id #uuid "3db431ad-aaac-579c-961c-14884ec8b4d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161063" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ and    \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $f={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2z{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161063/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161063"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection/ea7694e85e9729ddabbae65b4d31601c4f61dac3761ebfe8f8681422a75d081e" :answer/id #uuid "2c65c1ca-6332-5a59-af35-eb03f0897829" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e255beb6-b10b-567e-b66f-8ed34e10b62b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection/0ca7f4e318cbc1890f9d4f791b7cd21f2f65386fc7bc508715260df00230af0d" :answer/id #uuid "e131b8aa-f2cc-51bb-b13f-63ad0cf88bb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection/96b025386ecadd6598ec2de2b0e94b821ccf3596629dd4902fabe891abdc30e1" :answer/id #uuid "45c24d99-52d0-54c9-bf49-ab478c5b1f8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection/4ed96d7706a8f17c59b9df237aab969a863399dd75679482a23095a24ab9d988" :answer/id #uuid "57c5b7ff-eaa7-5818-b02d-10141731da15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "584e7127-31ef-5e9f-92c8-46f1b37a4dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161073/selection/0ca7f4e318cbc1890f9d4f791b7cd21f2f65386fc7bc508715260df00230af0d" :answer-field/choices ["q-161073/selection/ea7694e85e9729ddabbae65b4d31601c4f61dac3761ebfe8f8681422a75d081e" "q-161073/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-161073/selection/0ca7f4e318cbc1890f9d4f791b7cd21f2f65386fc7bc508715260df00230af0d" "q-161073/selection/96b025386ecadd6598ec2de2b0e94b821ccf3596629dd4902fabe891abdc30e1" "q-161073/selection/4ed96d7706a8f17c59b9df237aab969a863399dd75679482a23095a24ab9d988"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161073" :question/id #uuid "7b3145af-3a0e-5c06-85fe-84edbe5ec50f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161073" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\text{_______}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [1,4]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [1,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161073/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161073"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161069"] :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={cos}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd  component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={cos}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection/3e5fb4a476da99e416fa40a81d59ec873bd117d0974c6cc1e43329b0f79d6fb9" :answer/id #uuid "06f0db32-985b-516b-b7ef-42e7e4e4380b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection/7d4cf49149e29d2e46219e17133e8d0f024b8651718eb7acab051a72bbd3f3e1" :answer/id #uuid "c0172939-9364-5913-aa3b-6f0b7d98dcfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection/020aad5cd5336afcbb3e57681c6f2e4745d8173f35f96b0d90cbefd1cb2869ad" :answer/id #uuid "eecceef7-8490-5582-8b96-d2526d8041c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection/ccaae2dcfff6d9fd334d5bd03588be327107a8dc4ced20afcfe800bdc3070419" :answer/id #uuid "e8b4f2df-3b70-5b68-b392-34c9b7228b49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection/8be0e78d3ef58789ff2f0e708c32b578b7dcd8280ad84595842f1dfce5117ff5" :answer/id #uuid "a8f85f4d-2d12-5506-9264-d853cae57d66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x}{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c761b0-acf0-50ae-a4eb-a81b658081d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161093/selection/3e5fb4a476da99e416fa40a81d59ec873bd117d0974c6cc1e43329b0f79d6fb9" :answer-field/choices ["q-161093/selection/3e5fb4a476da99e416fa40a81d59ec873bd117d0974c6cc1e43329b0f79d6fb9" "q-161093/selection/7d4cf49149e29d2e46219e17133e8d0f024b8651718eb7acab051a72bbd3f3e1" "q-161093/selection/020aad5cd5336afcbb3e57681c6f2e4745d8173f35f96b0d90cbefd1cb2869ad" "q-161093/selection/ccaae2dcfff6d9fd334d5bd03588be327107a8dc4ced20afcfe800bdc3070419" "q-161093/selection/8be0e78d3ef58789ff2f0e708c32b578b7dcd8280ad84595842f1dfce5117ff5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161093" :question/id #uuid "3832d2fa-673b-5b53-98a7-cbe8b02e2624" :question/math-academy-id "q-161093" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161093/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106058"] :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=1+{y}^{2}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=1+{y}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1+{y}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2y.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection/e50e19110f31a584c66d2fb63fe03f2f1956b1f28882437d1d6c0a40d9e46895" :answer/id #uuid "5e934144-6c85-5e8c-bf19-73988ec05b48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection/350ee384e7e9d99ec2d137f6936e738631b8f2c1f981b0d59a7d15656440e46d" :answer/id #uuid "86c1a04f-830b-5a1f-97b9-70453b78a08c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection/980cc612f17d4b7137e2bd4009c889bc1b76d380428d528ab6669e435f408b0d" :answer/id #uuid "5d17dfea-8619-5088-b99e-44219f2a3d04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection/42423e167faeb270fd2f87c26aa1a67c7004ff9b0afba4d36a13c91b7aad2a93" :answer/id #uuid "b1e7e052-255f-5bd3-8480-4790c13f9566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection/c714823955509c61289c413baa5ffcd51890fb3842ad6540443b94db8be99701" :answer/id #uuid "2946b410-32b1-5d52-a4a8-6301339754e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77e0e4-0c2a-5bad-8b8a-fb682d469194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161099/selection/e50e19110f31a584c66d2fb63fe03f2f1956b1f28882437d1d6c0a40d9e46895" :answer-field/choices ["q-161099/selection/e50e19110f31a584c66d2fb63fe03f2f1956b1f28882437d1d6c0a40d9e46895" "q-161099/selection/350ee384e7e9d99ec2d137f6936e738631b8f2c1f981b0d59a7d15656440e46d" "q-161099/selection/980cc612f17d4b7137e2bd4009c889bc1b76d380428d528ab6669e435f408b0d" "q-161099/selection/42423e167faeb270fd2f87c26aa1a67c7004ff9b0afba4d36a13c91b7aad2a93" "q-161099/selection/c714823955509c61289c413baa5ffcd51890fb3842ad6540443b94db8be99701"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161099" :question/id #uuid "e85c59df-7be0-577e-b503-9d8d25c6e082" :question/math-academy-id "q-161099" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161099/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161099"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161095"] :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection/3ff71d88475a98d18d4e72cf009e33df99369f04a0294c50e81eca7855c11aea" :answer/id #uuid "633b3186-c1a4-570c-b30b-4d3ac8934d23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection/a8566ce6b8a613c54db1a1169aa8dda1d54ac8497fa7a437a61b1f7a7adf7cd8" :answer/id #uuid "e1661cd3-48da-5584-b957-23a1fbb9030d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{z}^{3}\\sqrt{1+z\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection/637db34add9a04d0348a4a504edffd43009ed934106699c486429cddbb8cf410" :answer/id #uuid "e18392ea-8064-5398-bf6f-b4a481622fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection/30f83c606f559591f6a31d39ac313e5772749f323a2acc5fed8aea12976c185f" :answer/id #uuid "f8a889d6-1cf3-559e-96ba-49e862b79b7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{2}\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection/8b22abfe85141139645ad5b9c7aea23f5e1b9f4d1a73b0b6d62ca7e2052636c0" :answer/id #uuid "ba67d8ba-fdbc-5d94-a6e8-fb880a165b3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "54935dcb-30ae-5216-8fbc-1bdd99135d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161097/selection/8b22abfe85141139645ad5b9c7aea23f5e1b9f4d1a73b0b6d62ca7e2052636c0" :answer-field/choices ["q-161097/selection/3ff71d88475a98d18d4e72cf009e33df99369f04a0294c50e81eca7855c11aea" "q-161097/selection/a8566ce6b8a613c54db1a1169aa8dda1d54ac8497fa7a437a61b1f7a7adf7cd8" "q-161097/selection/637db34add9a04d0348a4a504edffd43009ed934106699c486429cddbb8cf410" "q-161097/selection/30f83c606f559591f6a31d39ac313e5772749f323a2acc5fed8aea12976c185f" "q-161097/selection/8b22abfe85141139645ad5b9c7aea23f5e1b9f4d1a73b0b6d62ca7e2052636c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161097" :question/id #uuid "eb47bd6d-1b90-53a8-94d7-94b3a1b41796" :question/math-academy-id "q-161097" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=z\\sqrt{z}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=z\\sqrt{z}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161097/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161097"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8879"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in \\left[0,1\\right].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: x^2 \\cos\\theta, \\: x^2\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\nx \\in \\left[0, 1\\right],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8880"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\cos{y}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y \\in [0, \\pi].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y) = 1-\\cos{y} \\geq 0$ for all $y \\in [0, \\pi].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta) = \n\\big\\langle (1-\\cos{y}) \\cos\\theta, \\: \ny, \\: \n(1-\\cos{y}) \\sin\\theta \n\\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\ny \\in[0, \\pi],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12364"] :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=e^{x/2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=e^{x/2},$ and \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=e^{x/2},$ we obtain $f' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is $\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12365"] :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\sin z \\geq 0$ for all $z \\in [0, \\pi].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sin z,$ and\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (z,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi , \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\sin z,$ we obtain $f'(z) = \\cos z.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
